<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#oba-of-benin">Glossary</a> · Term · title</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oba of Benin</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oba</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba</a>, meaning 'king', is the traditional ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Kingdom of Benin</a> and the custodian of the culture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-people">Edo people</a> and all Edoid people. The Oba dynasty is named after its first ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eweka-i">Eweka I</a>, of around the 12th century, and followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ogiso-dynasty">Ogiso</a> monarchy; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-the-great">Ewuare the Great</a> (1440–1473) heads the list of the imperial period. In 1897 a British force of about 1,200 men under Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harry-rawson">Harry Rawson</a> captured, sacked and burned the capital, looted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a> from the palace and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovonramwen">Oba Ovonramwen</a> into exile; he died in 1914 without his throne. His line keeps the title as traditional rulers in modern-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ewuare-ii">Ewuare II</a> since 2016.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oba-of-benin.jpg" alt="Oba of Benin" width="313" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ovonramwen_Nogbaisi_with_his_two_wives_in_Calabar,_c1912_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
